WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.567
Variance.

2
00:00:01.867 --> 00:00:05.717
Two datasets can share a mean and still look completely different.

3
00:00:05.717 --> 00:00:07.700
Variance measures that difference.

4
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.733
Variance is the average squared distance from the mean.

5
00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:13.603
Squaring does two jobs.

6
00:00:13.603 --> 00:00:17.833
It removes the sign, so deviations below and above both count.

7
00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:21.033
And it punishes big deviations disproportionately.

8
00:00:22.233 --> 00:00:25.567
Let's compute it for three values: two, four, and six.

9
00:00:25.867 --> 00:00:29.767
The mean is four, so the deviations are minus two, zero, and two.

10
00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:32.101
Square each one: four, zero, four.

11
00:00:32.101 --> 00:00:34.733
Add them up to get eight, then average them.

12
00:00:35.933 --> 00:00:37.900
As a formula, it looks like this.

13
00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.900
Add up each point's squared deviation from the mean.

14
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:42.767
Then divide by the count.

15
00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:47.329
One price: the units are squared, kilometers squared for kilometer data.

16
00:00:47.329 --> 00:00:49.933
That's why we usually quote the square root.

17
00:00:51.133 --> 00:00:52.633
Now the classic gotcha.

18
00:00:52.933 --> 00:00:58.049
Dividing by n gives population variance, and NumPy does that by default.

19
00:00:58.049 --> 00:01:02.667
Dividing by n minus one gives sample variance, which pandas uses.

20
00:01:02.967 --> 00:01:07.867
For our example, eight over three is two point six seven, but eight over two is four.

21
00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:10.567
So always pass ddof explicitly.

22
00:01:11.767 --> 00:01:14.933
Here it is in code, with four planet orbital periods.

23
00:01:15.233 --> 00:01:19.000
We find the mean, then average the squared deviations by hand.

24
00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:22.367
It matches NumPy's population variance exactly.

25
00:01:22.667 --> 00:01:26.900
And the sample version, dividing by n minus one, comes out larger.

26
00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:30.300
Here's how this bites in real projects.

27
00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:35.133
Your training script standardizes features with NumPy, so it divides by n.

28
00:01:35.433 --> 00:01:40.000
Your evaluation notebook uses pandas, which divides by n minus one.

29
00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:45.172
Your identical pipelines now silently disagree on every scaled value.

30
00:01:45.172 --> 00:01:49.267
Pass ddof explicitly and you'll never lose that afternoon.

31
00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:51.165
Let's recap.

32
00:01:51.165 --> 00:01:54.367
Variance is the average squared distance from the mean.

33
00:01:54.667 --> 00:01:57.633
Squaring removes the sign and punishes big gaps.

34
00:01:57.933 --> 00:02:01.633
Its units are squared, so we usually quote the standard deviation.

35
00:02:01.933 --> 00:02:04.455
And always set ddof explicitly.

36
00:02:04.455 --> 00:02:06.733
Next up: Standard deviation.
